Accept "scheme:/host" as equivalent to "scheme://host" in url_parse()

Reverse proxies commonly sanitize request paths, collapsing duplicate
slashes. Traefik does this by default (entryPoint http.sanitizePath,
default true; documented as directory-traversal hardening).

hash-archive carries the target URL *inside* the request path, e.g.
    GET /history/https://example.com/foo.txt
so sanitization rewrites it to
    GET /history/https:/example.com/foo.txt
and url_parse() then fails: the "Absolute" branch scans for "://",
leaves out->host empty, and returns URL_EPARSE. The user sees HTTP 400
and the URL is never hashed. This breaks the web form, the /history/
route and scripted clients (e.g. the R package `contentid`) alike.

Percent-encoding is not a workaround: url_parse() does no percent
decoding, so "https:%2F%2Fexample.com" is treated literally and fails
identically with or without a proxy.

Fix: in the Absolute branch only, when the "://" scan yields no host,
retry once with a single slash. This is reached solely for input that
would otherwise be rejected outright, so no URL that parses today can
change meaning. Fields are reset before the retry because sscanf leaves
partially-populated output on a failed match.

Upstream is unmaintained (last commit 2021-10-31), so this is carried
as a local patch rather than sent upstream.

--- a/src/util/url.c
+++ b/src/util/url.c
@@ -40,7 +40,21 @@
 		sscanf(URL, URL_SCHEME_FMT "://" URL_HOST_FMT URL_PATH_FMT URL_QUERY_FMT,
 			out->scheme, out->host, out->path, out->query);
 		if('\0' == out->scheme[0]) return URL_EPARSE;
-		if('\0' == out->host[0]) return URL_EPARSE;
+		if('\0' == out->host[0]) {
+			// Retry with a single slash. A proxy that sanitizes request
+			// paths collapses "//" to "/", so "https://example.com/x"
+			// arrives as "https:/example.com/x". Only reached when the
+			// two-slash form already failed, so this cannot alter the
+			// parse of any URL that is currently accepted.
+			out->scheme[0] = '\0';
+			out->host[0] = '\0';
+			out->path[0] = '\0';
+			out->query[0] = '\0';
+			sscanf(URL, URL_SCHEME_FMT ":/" URL_HOST_FMT URL_PATH_FMT URL_QUERY_FMT,
+				out->scheme, out->host, out->path, out->query);
+			if('\0' == out->scheme[0]) return URL_EPARSE;
+			if('\0' == out->host[0]) return URL_EPARSE;
+		}
 	}
 	if('\0' != out->path[0] && '/' != out->path[0]) return URL_EPARSE;
 	if('\0' != out->query[0] && '?' != out->query[0]) return URL_EPARSE;
